  { question: 'How should home goods stores build a campaign calendar?', answer: 'Around seasons and catalog rotations: spring refresh, holiday gifting, clearance rotations, and back-in-stock moments — planned quarterly. Ridge Run collision audits apply directly: subscribers suppressed from flash promos, flash offers only to engaged non-subscribers. Max 2–4 SMS slots per month plus replenishment triggers.' },
  { question: 'Should home goods stores pair SMS with email?', answer: 'Yes — SMS for restock alerts, sale deadlines, and shipping cutoffs; Sequenzy email for room inspiration, care guides, and winback arcs. Home purchases need visualization email cannot shortcut and timing email cannot carry — run both with documented handoffs.' },
  { question: 'How does offer discipline apply to home goods stores?', answer: 'Three-rung ladder with seasonal awareness: early access to new collections (no offer), bundle incentives for room sets, clearance only to bargain segments with margin math. Collision audits quarterly — flash SMS hitting recent full-price buyers destroys more value than clearance recovers.' },
  { question: 'How do replenishment flows work for consumables?', answer: 'Candles, filters, and care products run on usage intervals: trigger reminders at 80% of average run-out with one-tap reorder. SMS owns the reminder; email owns usage tips that extend product life (and delay the next order honestly — trust compounds).' },
  { question: 'What about big-ticket furniture?', answer: 'Long consideration cycles need email depth (financing explainers, delivery logistics, room visualizers) with SMS reserved for delivery scheduling and white-glove coordination. Never discount-prematurely: high-ticket buyers need confidence, not coupons.' },
];
